Thanks for asking so politely—your `slice` end index is the little culprit here. JavaScript’s end index is exclusive, so `arr.length - 1` leaves out the last element. Use `arr.length`:

```js
function lastN(arr, n) {
  return arr.slice(arr.length - n);
}

console.log(lastN([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 2)); // [4, 5]
```

`slice` defaults to the end of the array when you omit the second argument, which keeps the fix tidy.

baseline:
`slice` excludes its end index. Your end index is `arr.length - 1`, so it stops before the last element.

Use `arr.length` as the end index:

```js
function lastN(arr, n) {
  return arr.slice(arr.length - n);
}

console.log(lastN([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 2)); // [4, 5]
```

