`data.users` is `undefined`, so `.map()` has nothing to run on. If a missing user list should count as empty, guard it with a fallback:

```js
fetch('/api/users')
  .then((r) => r.json())
  .then((data) => {
    const names = (data.users ?? []).map((u) => u.name);
    render(names);
  });
```

If `users` is required by the API, check the response shape instead—the fallback can hide a server response problem. The bug is a tiny mismatch between what the code expects and what the API returned.

The response nests `users` inside `data`, so `data.users` is undefined. Use `data.data.users`:

```js
fetch('/api/users')
  .then((r) => r.json())
  .then((response) => {
    const names = response.data.users.map((u) => u.name);
    render(names);
  });
```

If that field might be missing in some responses, use `(response.data?.users ?? []).map(...)`. The API added an extra wrapper layer; that’s the part the first fix couldn’t guess.

